    In essence, a vertex i is in the independent set if the output of the corresponding polynomial
fi is at least 1, and it is not in the independent set if the output of fi is at most 1/2. We allow up
to η n “errors”, i.e., vertices i which satisfy one of the following:

   • either fi (A) ∈ (1/2, 1), or

   • there is some edge e containing i such that e ⊆ I (i violates the independence constraint).

The error tolerance of (1/2, 1) is important for our impossibility results, as this ensures that a small
change in f (A, ω) cannot cause a large change in the resulting independent set without encountering
the failure event ∅. With the mapping Vfη in hand, let us formally define how a random polynomial
finds an independent set of a certain size.


                                                    8
Definition 1.2. For parameters k > 0, δ ∈ [0, 1], and ξ ≥ 1, a random function f : Rm → Rn
is said to (k, δ, ξ, η)-optimize the independent set problem in Hr (n, p) if the following are satisfied
when A ∼ Hr (n, p):

   • EA,ω ∥f (A, ω)∥2 ≤ ξk and
                      

         h                i
   • PA,ω |Vfη (A, ω)| ≥ k ≥ 1 − δ.

    Here, k is the desired size of the independent set, δ is the failure probability of the algorithm,
ξ is a normalization parameter, and η is the error tolerance parameter of the rounding procedure.
Similarly, we define how a random polynomial finds a balanced independent set of a certain size.

Definition 1.3. For parameters k1 , . . . , kr > 0, δ ∈ [0, 1], and ξ ≥ 1, a random function f : Rm →
Rrn is said to (k1 , . . . , kr , δ, ξ, η)-optimize the balanced independent set problem in H(r, n, p) if the
following are satisfied when A ∼ H(r, n, p):

   • EA,ω ∥f (A, ω)∥2 ≤ ξ(k1 + · · · + kr ) and
