Proof of Lemma 4.1. We will employ the so-called “vertex-based” approach toward applying The-
orem 4.2 to problems in random hypergraphs. First, we partition the edges of Kr×n into rn′ groups
{Eij : 1 ≤ i ≤ r, 1 ≤ j ≤ n′ }. We include (v1 , . . . , vr ) ∈ Eij if and only if the following hold for
1 ≤ l ≤ r:

   • vl ∈ Pl,jl for some jl > j if l < i, and

   • vl ∈ Pl,jl for some jl ≥ j if l ≥ i.

In other words, an edge e = (v1 , . . . , vr ) is assigned to Eij where j is the minimum “partition
number” of a vertex v ∈ e, and i is the minimum value l such that vl is in Pl,j .
     For each 1 ≤ i ≤ r and 1 ≤ j ≤ n′ , we let Zij denote the outcomes of the edges in Eij . Clearly,
the variables Zij are independent. Furthermore, we claim that changing the outcomes of the edges
in a single Eij can change the value β(·) by at most q. We will prove this claim by considering a
few cases. To this end, let Z⃗ denote an arbitrary outcome of the Zij ’s, let H denote the hypergraph
determined by these outcomes, and let I be the γ-balanced P -independent set in H satisfying
|I| = β(H). Let Z ⃗ ′ be obtained from Z⃗ by changing the outcomes of the edges in Eij for some i, j,
let H ′ be the corresponding hypergraph and let I ′ be the largest γ-balanced P -independent set in
H ′.

      (Case 1) I ∩ Pi,j = ∅. Note that I is still a γ-balanced P -independent set in H ′ and so
|I ′ | ≥ |I|. If I ′ ∩ Pi,j = ∅, then I ′ is a γ-balanced P -independent set in H and so |I ′ | = |I|.
Suppose |I ′ ∩ Pi,j | = 1. Then, there exists a γ-balanced P -independent set J ′ ⊆ I ′ in H ′ such that
|J ′ | ≥ |I ′ | − q and J ′ ∩ Pi,j = ∅. As J ′ is also a γ-balanced P -independent set in H, it follows that
|J ′ | ≤ |I|. In particular, |I ′ | ≤ |I| + q, as desired.

    (Case 2) |I ∩ Pi,j | = 1. Note that there exists a γ-balanced P -independent set J ⊆ I in H such
that |J| ≥ |I| − q and J ∩ Pi,j = ∅. As J is a γ-balanced P -independent set in H ′ , it follows that
|J| ≤ |I ′ |. In particular, |I ′ | ≥ |I| − q. If I ′ ∩ Pi,j = ∅, then I ′ is a γ-balanced P -independent set in
H, implying |I ′ | ≤ |I|. If not, an identical argument to the previous case shows that |I ′ | ≤ |I| + q,
as desired.

